00:01:03.584 --> 00:01:07.827
It's actually, the Neanderthal
report is kind of an example

00:01:07.827 --> 00:01:10.526
of some of the things that we've done.

00:01:10.526 --> 00:01:15.657
Where we try to make genetics, and frankly
try to make all of science relatable,

00:01:15.657 --> 00:01:18.384
make it something that's fun for people.

00:01:18.384 --> 00:01:22.007
So honestly, one of the first things,
I was at a TED dinner once and

00:01:22.007 --> 00:01:26.606
we were talking about, there was Craig
Venter, all these famous genetics people,

00:01:26.606 --> 00:01:29.297
and they were talking to
a bunch of tech leaders.

00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:33.229
And they were talking about the latest
results in one area or another.

00:01:33.229 --> 00:01:36.475
And then I brought up
something about asparagus and

00:01:36.475 --> 00:01:39.060
how you can smell it in your pee.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.626
And 45 minutes later, people were
still talking about the asparagus and

00:01:42.626 --> 00:01:43.620
the pee question.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.690
And I realized people want scientific
information that's kind of fun,

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:52.240
kind of the cocktail party conversation,
and something that's interesting.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.597
So Neanderthals was absolutely
one of the interesting parts.

00:01:55.597 --> 00:01:57.620
It teaches people about science.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.978
And then it does become one of those
things that people are always comparing.

00:02:00.978 --> 00:02:03.680
