what does that mean?

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:04.910
And what should I do about it?

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.739
So it's interesting.

00:02:06.739 --> 00:02:09.152
But no,
it's a proud fact that you should post.

00:02:09.152 --> 00:02:10.540
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Widely on everything.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:11.965
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you.

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.452
I hope you can all find your-
&gt;&gt; I think that's why you got in [LAUGH].

00:02:14.452 --> 00:02:17.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, actually I think so, thank you.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.745
[LAUGH] Well,
since we're already talking about 23andMe,

00:02:20.745 --> 00:02:23.080
I'd love to dig in a little bit more.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.870
So I'm amongst, many of us here
are amongst the 12 million users.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.043
And I'd like to take us back to day one,
actually.

00:02:31.043 --> 00:02:36.227
How did you convince that initial set
of customers to buy a DNA ancestry kit?

00:02:37.921 --> 00:02:40.355
&gt;&gt; Well,
we didn't convince very many people.

00:02:40.355 --> 00:02:45.733
[LAUGH] We had, I'd say one of the things
that's great about 23andMe, and that was

00:02:45.733 --> 00:02:51.770
also a shortcoming in those early days,
we're incredibly strong on the science.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.856
And I had two scientific
co-founders from Stanford,

00:02:54.856 --> 00:02:57.440
who had just finished their PhDs.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.580
And they had joined, and we created,
essentially, the product we dreamed of.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.142
How can I peruse my chromosomes and

00:03:05.142 --> 00:03:10.277
look at all the different reports and
see what they mean and explore?

00:03:10.277 --> 00:03:12.927
It was kind of academic fantasies.

00:03:12.927 --> 00:03:16.739
And we have a product now
called Ancestry Composition.

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:19.610
In the early days we had these PCA plots,
and

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.660
it was relatively technical and
hard to see.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.032
And we thought it was all great.

00:03:25.032 --> 00:03:28.721
And so we didn't know marketing research,
at all.

00:03:28.721 --> 00:03:33.804
So from a business school perspective,
we did not asses market demand.

00:03:33.804 --> 00:03:37.567
We just kind of made the hypothesis,
of course, everyone wants their genome,

00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:39.600
of course everyone's
going to want to get it.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.277
So it was, academically,
very interesting and it was controversial.

00:03:44.277 --> 00:03:45.993
So we were the front cover,

00:03:45.993 --> 00:03:50.485
we were above the fold front page of
The New York Times when we launched.

00:03:50.485 --> 00:03:52.024
Amy Harmon wrote the article.

00:03:52.024 --> 00:03:54.884
It was the only time New York Times
has ever written sort of

00:03:54.884 --> 00:03:57.930
a first-person account
of them doing a product.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.330
We were on the cover of Wired,
Thomas Goetz wrote that piece.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:04.610
So we got tons of coverage.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:08.401
And the first day we sold 1,000 kits.

00:04:08.401 --> 00:04:15.670
And after that,
it trickled down to 10 to 15 kits a day.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:19.839
And we didn't have any marketing team.

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:21.807
We just kind of assumed it
would be word of mouth.

00:04:21.807 --> 00:04:26.570
It was $1,000 and the value
proposition was not clearly there.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.487
It was really sort of an academic product.

00:04:28.487 --> 00:04:32.209
So that was kind of the first
moment in the company we were like,

00:04:32.209 --> 00:04:36.217
we might have to do a little bit
more product market research here,

00:04:36.217 --> 00:04:38.738
to figure out what's going to drive sales.

00:04:38.738 --> 00:04:43.042
But I think the first customers that we
got were really people who are sort of

00:04:43.042 --> 00:04:44.820
the quantified self-world,

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.600
the people who just want to
test themselves on everything.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.908
And I would say, there's a market of
50 to 100,000 people who are just like,

00:04:52.908 --> 00:04:54.290
I want to try it all.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:57.009
And those were the people
who were coming our way,

00:04:57.009 --> 00:04:59.810
who happened to also
have the spare $1,000.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:04.740
But it was pretty brutal,
the first few years.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's very interesting.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.740
And so now, you found product-market fit,
you found all these amazing customers.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.851
&gt;&gt; Well, I would say we're still
finding product-market fit.

00:05:15.851 --> 00:05:20.366
I mean, I think what's interesting for
me with genetics, and

00:05:20.366 --> 00:05:24.540
I don't know healthcare,
it's so interesting because GSB,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.135
you guys have founded so
many amazing companies.

00:05:28.135 --> 00:05:30.489
And the tech world has
really come from here.

00:05:30.489 --> 00:05:33.849
And there's all kinds of areas that
have been really revolutionized.

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:39.567
But healthcare is this last frontier
that has not really been evolved.

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:43.720
And the reality is,
it's not just like it's this frontier.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:45.910
It's kind of like
a different solar system.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.016
Healthcare doesn't
operate in the same way.

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:54.680
We all use healthcare, but
none of us are the customers.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.552
You're just the means for
generating revenue, but

00:05:57.552 --> 00:05:59.890
somebody else is paying the bill.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.430
So it's really challenging to change.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.281
And it's also incredibly fragmented.

00:06:05.281 --> 00:06:09.892
So in the tech world, when there's
a new technology that comes out,

00:06:09.892 --> 00:06:12.128
it's usually rapidly adopted.

00:06:12.128 --> 00:06:16.116
You look at what's happened with
machine learning, and TensorFlow, and

00:06:16.116 --> 00:06:20.055
all these things that Google puts out,
it gets adopted relatively fast.

00:06:20.055 --> 00:06:25.565
And the human genome was
sequenced 20 years ago.

00:06:25.565 --> 00:06:30.748
And I would say, how many people have
had their doctor recommend that they

00:06:30.748 --> 00:06:36.190
get their genome and use it outside
of pregnancy or outside of cancer?

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:37.591
It's almost none.

00:06:37.591 --> 00:06:39.621
And so
part of the issue we have right now,

00:06:39.621 --> 00:06:42.704
is genetics has still not been
adopted by the existing system.

00:06:42.704 --> 00:06:44.583
And there's all kinds of laws for
that reason, though.

00:06:44.583 --> 00:06:48.085
So for instance, doctors,
there's no profit motive for

00:06:48.085 --> 00:06:50.092
people to get this information.

00:06:50.092 --> 00:06:54.591
So a doctor does not make money
if they give a diagnostic.

00:06:54.591 --> 00:06:56.254
And so there's good things about that.

00:06:56.254 --> 00:06:59.631
You don't want them excessively
recommending, but for that reason,

00:06:59.631 --> 00:07:03.470
as well, no one is really motivated
to create extra work for themselves.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:09.230
So when I think about product-market fit,
there's still this huge chasm between,

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:13.630
I have lots of consumers who
are frankly really eager to do more and

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.200
take more ownership of their health.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:20.331
But the healthcare world's really
not set up in any capacity

00:07:20.331 --> 00:07:22.860
to help them execute on that.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.476
And so,
doctors are not trained on genetics,

00:07:25.476 --> 00:07:29.112
doctors are not used to people
coming in with more information.

00:07:29.112 --> 00:07:35.208
And frankly prevention is not
a reimbursed part of medicine, it's not.

00:07:35.208 --> 00:07:37.258
Figuring out how you can exercise or

00:07:37.258 --> 00:07:41.440
how you should eat better is not
something that you get from your doctor.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:42.950
You get that on your own.

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:47.502
But frankly, that's a huge component
of all kinds of prevention.

00:07:47.502 --> 00:07:50.103
So we're still working on
product-market fit, and

00:07:50.103 --> 00:07:53.761
how you actually create a new industry
that frankly is direct to consumer.

00:07:53.761 --> 00:07:57.344
And how you're inspiring people to take
more ownership of their own health,

00:07:57.344 --> 00:08:00.278
but it's also an out-of-pocket expense.

00:08:00.278 --> 00:08:04.556
Yes, I think that's a very humble answer,
you have [LAUGH] 12 million customers,

00:08:04.556 --> 00:08:05.555
which is amazing.

00:08:05.555 --> 00:08:08.003
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I'd like to talk about those 12

00:08:08.003 --> 00:08:12.393
million, because obviously selling
kits is one part of the business, but

00:08:12.393 --> 00:08:15.611
then when you have these
12 million plus customers.

00:08:15.611 --> 00:08:19.674
There's a swath of data, of course,
that comes along with that,

00:08:19.674 --> 00:08:22.881
and massive opportunity
in the therapeutic space.

00:08:22.881 --> 00:08:27.334
So I'm curious as you're moving
to focusing really on that data,

00:08:27.334 --> 00:08:30.206
what gets you excited about all that data?

00:08:30.206 --> 00:08:35.978
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean that for me,
when I think about starting the company,

00:08:35.978 --> 00:08:40.246
that was a huge part of my incentive and
my dreams.

00:08:40.246 --> 00:08:47.014
I find it fascinating that all of
life comes from a really simple code.

00:08:47.014 --> 00:08:50.538
So for those of you who are engineers or
coders,

00:08:50.538 --> 00:08:55.386
it's mind-blowing to me how
remarkably simple, you have an A,

00:08:55.386 --> 00:08:59.903
C, G and T, and that accounts for
all of life on this planet.

00:08:59.903 --> 00:09:03.171
And it's small variations, you, and

00:09:03.171 --> 00:09:08.221
all of us are 99.5% the same,
and I always tell my kids,

00:09:08.221 --> 00:09:12.589
you're 50% banana, and you're 90% mouse.

00:09:12.589 --> 00:09:15.555
So you're very similar to everything.

00:09:15.555 --> 00:09:20.975
It's remarkable to me that we
don't understand this code.

00:09:20.975 --> 00:09:27.613
So I think about, every night you go to
bed and your body just, everything works,

00:09:27.613 --> 00:09:33.889
you live in this incredibly complex
system, and we have no idea how it works.

00:09:33.889 --> 00:09:38.552
To me, this is the ultimate puzzle,
it's really, as a kid,

00:09:38.552 --> 00:09:42.964
it used to just keep me up at night,
how does it all happen?

00:09:42.964 --> 00:09:47.947
And even things like how all of
your cells can differentiate, and

00:09:47.947 --> 00:09:51.579
how they turn out,
it's mind-blowing to me.

00:09:51.579 --> 00:09:53.955
So by having a large amount of data,

00:09:53.955 --> 00:09:58.243
we're starting to understand
what does the human genome mean.

00:09:58.243 --> 00:10:01.712
And you have some really simple examples.

00:10:01.712 --> 00:10:08.055
So for instance, one mutation causes
whether or not you have dry earwax,

00:10:08.055 --> 00:10:12.675
or wet earwax,
another really fun medical example.

00:10:12.675 --> 00:10:17.110
But the reason why we put out the dry
earwax and the wet earwax kind of report,

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:18.922
is because it teaches people.

00:10:18.922 --> 00:10:22.067
That you can have one simple
genetic variation, and

00:10:22.067 --> 00:10:25.658
that can cause a different phenotype,
a different trait.

00:10:25.658 --> 00:10:28.314
And so what's really interesting for me,

00:10:28.314 --> 00:10:32.740
is by having almost 13 million people,
and billions of data points,

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:36.386
is that we're starting to make
sense of the human genome.

00:10:36.386 --> 00:10:41.037
And frankly, this should translate one
day, when you have billions and billions,

00:10:41.037 --> 00:10:42.674
it should translate one day,

00:10:42.674 --> 00:10:45.773
is how you actually really
understand the code of life.

00:10:45.773 --> 00:10:49.655
And that's really for me the ultimate goal
here, is like you want to understand it.

00:10:49.655 --> 00:10:51.957
And when I think about drug discovery,

00:10:51.957 --> 00:10:55.949
my background when I was investing
on Wall Street, was in biotech.

00:10:55.949 --> 00:11:00.279
And biotech, it's amazing because
the science is so interesting,

00:11:00.279 --> 00:11:02.777
but from an investing perspective.

00:11:02.777 --> 00:11:07.556
There was one well-known investor
who's like, I love biotech, he's like,

00:11:07.556 --> 00:11:10.569
90% of everything fails,
I just short it all.

00:11:10.569 --> 00:11:14.123
And [LAUGH] that's true from
an investment perspective,

00:11:14.123 --> 00:11:18.723
there's some reality to that,
statistically, most it's going to fail.

00:11:18.723 --> 00:11:21.071
But for me, it's like I want to
create things that are going to work,

00:11:21.071 --> 00:11:23.777
I want to create things that are actually
going to have an impact on people.

00:11:23.777 --> 00:11:26.107
So if you start with human genetics,

00:11:26.107 --> 00:11:29.350
you are more than twice as
likely to be successful.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:33.832
So one of my hopes here was,
to bring structure and

00:11:33.832 --> 00:11:37.587
a new kind of approach to drug discovery.

00:11:37.587 --> 00:11:40.332
So more of those drugs
would actually succeed.

00:11:40.332 --> 00:11:43.416
And again,
I think about my dad's a physicist,

00:11:43.416 --> 00:11:46.133
we mentioned my dad's particle physics.

00:11:46.133 --> 00:11:49.789
My dad used to always complain,
he's like, you biologist, it's so

00:11:49.789 --> 00:11:54.217
confusing, you're such a disaster, it's
all over the place, there's no structure.

00:11:54.217 --> 00:11:59.008
And there's some truth to it, it's really
complicated, and physics has its laws and

00:11:59.008 --> 00:12:00.001
its principles.

00:12:00.001 --> 00:12:04.586
So for me, that's part of the goal,
is in some ways once we have enough data,

00:12:04.586 --> 00:12:09.387
how do you bring enough structure so that
then you could really have very rational

00:12:09.387 --> 00:12:13.578
drug design, and actually be able
to have a much higher success rate?

00:12:13.578 --> 00:12:18.184
&gt;&gt; One unique aspect of the data
that you have is there is a self

00:12:18.184 --> 00:12:20.164
selecting aspect to it.

00:12:20.164 --> 00:12:23.579
A lot of most of the data that you have is
people who have signed up for the kits.

00:12:23.579 --> 00:12:27.076
So, how do you think about that
when you're making pretty large

00:12:27.076 --> 00:12:30.652
generalizations, potentially,
about the data that you have?

00:12:30.652 --> 00:12:33.126
&gt;&gt; Well, we get this a lot about,
what's our bias?

00:12:33.126 --> 00:12:37.780
And again, coming from Wall Street and
looking at all clinical trials, and say,

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:40.829
I've never seen a data set
that doesn't have a bias.

00:12:40.829 --> 00:12:45.658
So it's not bad that we have a bias, the
important thing is to know what it is, and

00:12:45.658 --> 00:12:48.937
to be able to identify it,
and then you account for it.

00:12:48.937 --> 00:12:53.601
So I would also say now that we are so
large,

00:12:53.601 --> 00:12:58.267
we have pretty amazing representation and

00:12:58.267 --> 00:13:03.073
diversity of all types
within the data set.

00:13:03.073 --> 00:13:08.470
And that was part of the push we had for
10 million, at 10 million

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:14.162
you get a lot of statistical power to
do analysis on most disease areas.

00:13:14.162 --> 00:13:18.879
So there's absolutely always a bias,
because we are self selecting,

00:13:18.879 --> 00:13:22.821
as people who are paying,
lowest entry point is $100.

00:13:22.821 --> 00:13:26.874
But we do attract people for
very different reasons, and

00:13:26.874 --> 00:13:31.095
I would say the Ancestry customers
which also do consent for

00:13:31.095 --> 00:13:34.219
research coming in at
a lower price point and

00:13:34.219 --> 00:13:39.724
are also interested in a different type
of product than my health customers.

00:13:39.724 --> 00:13:44.694
So I would say again, my main thing is
with data is it's important in some ways

00:13:44.694 --> 00:13:49.217
just to go for size, size really is
the number one thing that matters.

00:13:49.217 --> 00:13:52.200
And secondly is really
understanding your limitations, but

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.908
not like there's no such
thing as the perfect dataset.

00:13:54.908 --> 00:13:59.724
&gt;&gt; Right, well your intention around
women, and other disadvantaged groups, and

00:13:59.724 --> 00:14:04.400
getting data around them is so important,
because there are not enough medical

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:07.366
studies done on women and
people in other groups.

00:14:07.366 --> 00:14:10.819
And so is that something that
you think about as well as.

00:14:10.819 --> 00:14:15.258
&gt;&gt; We do, we think a lot about,
I mean most genetic studies have been

00:14:15.258 --> 00:14:19.883
done in European populations, so
that's one of the other things.

00:14:19.883 --> 00:14:23.117
We are still majority
European within 23andMe.

00:14:23.117 --> 00:14:27.624
But the fact that we are 20,
30% of 23andMe is non European,

00:14:27.624 --> 00:14:32.386
that makes us the largest in the world
of non European genetic studies.

00:14:32.386 --> 00:14:37.299
So there's things that we can do,
that absolutely will attract and

00:14:37.299 --> 00:14:40.500
encourage certain populations to come in.

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:45.400
And I think that's something that's more
and more of a priority for 23andMe is to

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:49.390
make sure that we're attracting
not just a European population but

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:50.940
a very broad population.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:56.412
And just to emphasize, I think people
don't realize, in some ways studying

00:14:56.412 --> 00:15:01.883
Africa is one of the most important and
exciting opportunities in genetics,

00:15:01.883 --> 00:15:07.630
because there's more diversity in Africa
than there is in the rest of the world.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:11.666
And so there's going to be a tremendous
number of really interesting genetic

00:15:11.666 --> 00:15:13.404
insights that come from there.

00:15:13.404 --> 00:15:18.175
So we're reaching a point, there's
been all kinds of challenges before,

00:15:18.175 --> 00:15:21.837
with the complexity of studying
different populations.

00:15:21.837 --> 00:15:25.150
But we're getting to a point
where we actually really have

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:28.273
that opportunity to do a lot
more in every population.

00:15:28.273 --> 00:15:31.593
And we think about ourselves
even as we put out a report,

00:15:31.593 --> 00:15:36.046
how do we hold ourselves to a bar that
we're not just putting out content for

00:15:36.046 --> 00:15:40.944
a European population, but putting out
content for all different communities.

00:15:40.944 --> 00:15:44.386
&gt;&gt; I'd like to go back to what you
were saying about marketing, and

00:15:44.386 --> 00:15:46.511
how it wasn't initially a huge focus.

00:15:46.511 --> 00:15:47.751
And in 2013,

00:15:47.751 --> 00:15:54.220
you unexpectedly got a note from the FDA
to discontinue marketing of the tests.

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:57.897
We do a lot of crisis management
case studies here at the GSB and

00:15:57.897 --> 00:16:03.545
I'm curious if you did, who was your first
call and what advice do they have for you?

00:16:03.545 --> 00:16:04.427
&gt;&gt; Good question.

00:16:04.427 --> 00:16:09.200
I love, so it's definitely one of the
parts when I speak at Rob Siegel's class

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:11.470
it's like one of the key things.

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:16.494
I love him asking the students like was it
Silicon Valley arrogance or ineptitude?

00:16:16.494 --> 00:16:19.185
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I love always hearing people talk about

00:16:19.185 --> 00:16:23.121
us and I just did another talk,
the innovators dilemma where also,

00:16:23.121 --> 00:16:26.668
there was really this sense is like,
you were so inept.

00:16:26.668 --> 00:16:34.321
[LAUGH] And so, what people don't realize
on 23andMe, we did get a warning letter.

00:16:34.321 --> 00:16:38.277
Mom was proud because it was one of
the most well read warning letters ever.

00:16:38.277 --> 00:16:40.821
[LAUGH] It was definitely, and

00:16:40.821 --> 00:16:46.435
partly it was it was an unusual warning
letter because it was so angry.

00:16:46.435 --> 00:16:50.710
What's interesting is when
23andMe was started like I said,

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.300
we were very academic.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:58.233
And one of, because we were academic, we
actually had Joanna Mountain who came from

00:16:58.233 --> 00:17:01.110
Stanford, she was
an assistant professor here.

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:06.922
We had an IRB, we thought through a lot,
like we engaged with ethics like

00:17:06.922 --> 00:17:12.830
Hank Reilly who still doesn't really
love us, but we engaged with him.

00:17:12.830 --> 00:17:16.775
I come from an academic background,
I love debate.

00:17:16.775 --> 00:17:18.940
Like tell me more, what can I do?

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:20.170
Where am I wrong?

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:22.210
So, we totally engaged.

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:25.997
And one of the first people we
had called was David Kessler,

00:17:25.997 --> 00:17:31.327
who was the former commissioner of the FDA
so he's like clearly very experienced.

00:17:31.327 --> 00:17:35.439
He was a professor at UCSF and
he pulled together his whole team,

00:17:35.439 --> 00:17:39.647
like his senior advisors from when
he was in the administration.

00:17:39.647 --> 00:17:43.167
And we sat down and
we're like are we a medical device?

00:17:43.167 --> 00:17:44.464
And we really debated this.

00:17:44.464 --> 00:17:48.549
Like it was a whole debate with his team,
like what are we, and

00:17:48.549 --> 00:17:51.970
concluded we're not a medical device.

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:54.152
And in those early days,
it was kind of a question.

00:17:54.152 --> 00:17:57.640
Like you have the right
to own your genome.

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:01.087
Like it's your data, like same way you
have a right to look in a mirror, that's

00:18:01.087 --> 00:18:04.753
your image, you have the right to have it,
so you have the right to have your genome.

00:18:04.753 --> 00:18:08.513
And in my mind all I was doing
was connecting you to publicly

00:18:08.513 --> 00:18:12.220
available literature that
seemed totally reasonable.

00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:17.282
So in those early days,
we conclude we're not a medical device and

00:18:17.282 --> 00:18:21.246
then we even met with people like
Von Eschenbach who was actually the FDA

00:18:21.246 --> 00:18:25.542
commissioner at the time and he was super
clear you are not a medical device and

00:18:25.542 --> 00:18:28.219
we will not be touching you and
regulating you.

00:18:28.219 --> 00:18:32.006
And one thing that was kind of
a political wake up call and

00:18:32.006 --> 00:18:36.366
just a company wake up call is
that when Obama came into office,

00:18:36.366 --> 00:18:41.430
that changes everything, and
the leadership of the FDA changed.

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:47.284
And they said, no, you are not
outside of our reign of regulation,

00:18:47.284 --> 00:18:49.659
we are going to regulate you.

00:18:49.659 --> 00:18:52.850
And so it was an overnight kind of shift.

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:57.277
Like we had just been told for years that
we were not going to be regulated and

00:18:57.277 --> 00:19:01.414
then it became very clear like you're
not just going to be regulated,

00:19:01.414 --> 00:19:02.920
like were coming now.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:05.977
So, I got the letter on a Friday and

00:19:05.977 --> 00:19:12.100
then I had the weekend to sort of
think about it and my typical stance.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:15.226
I was like, it's not such a big deal.

00:19:15.226 --> 00:19:20.516
[LAUGH] And then they made the letter
public, and Kessler called me.

00:19:20.516 --> 00:19:22.648
And he called me and
he said Anne, I know you, and

00:19:22.648 --> 00:19:24.345
I know you're not paying attention.

00:19:24.345 --> 00:19:25.096
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:19:25.096 --> 00:19:29.040
I'm telling you now that it's a big deal.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:36.900
And it was, the company really had to
pivot and I had no shortage of people.

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:40.140
Like there was a article Fortune
row called 23 and stupid.

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:44.551
I had no shortage of people,
like well known geneticists who are like,

00:19:44.551 --> 00:19:48.240
it's time for you to step back and
let us clean up your mess.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:53.268
Like no shortage of people who love to
come and be like the woman with no degree,

00:19:53.268 --> 00:19:55.188
I was really screwing up the industry.

00:19:55.188 --> 00:20:00.710
And I had one really great mentor,
and it was a regulator also a woman,

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:05.774
who said any of two choices,
like do you want to sell the company?

00:20:05.774 --> 00:20:06.928
I was like no.

00:20:06.928 --> 00:20:11.228
She was like well, then, you put your
head down and you just do the work.

00:20:11.228 --> 00:20:13.985
Get the work done, she like but
am telling you now,

00:20:13.985 --> 00:20:16.800
it's going to take you five to 10 years.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:21.372
And we were lucky because we had cash,
we had that ability, I found amazing

00:20:21.372 --> 00:20:25.885
leader to do it, and frankly,
it was the best thing for the company.

00:20:25.885 --> 00:20:29.980
because I think one of the most important
things 23andMe has proven out is that

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:33.580
consumers actually can
get genetic information.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:38.504
And most healthcare providers I would say,
tend to assume that you're not capable

00:20:38.504 --> 00:20:42.810
of changing your behavior or
understanding complicated information.

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:46.880
And we have now successfully
really proven out that you

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.390
can understand really complex concepts.

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:55.637
And we had to prove to the FDA
that at a certain grade level,

00:20:55.637 --> 00:20:59.810
over 90% could pass actually tests.

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:02.896
And we had to prove that with our
reports they can do that and we did.

00:21:02.896 --> 00:21:08.220
And we now have six,
seven FDA authorizations.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:11.353
We're the only ones who have
gone down this whole path.

00:21:11.353 --> 00:21:16.045
And I feel like when I think about us as
advocates, one of 23andMe as an activist

00:21:16.045 --> 00:21:20.668
brand, like we are advocates for consumers
and proving out that you are far more

00:21:20.668 --> 00:21:25.310
capable of taking care of yourself than
the healthcare system today believes.

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:28.355
&gt;&gt; And as you've just described,

00:21:28.355 --> 00:21:31.213
healthcare is obviously
a heavily regulated industry.

00:21:31.213 --> 00:21:31.772
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And

00:21:31.772 --> 00:21:36.297
many of us will be going into other
heavily regulated industries whether it's

00:21:36.297 --> 00:21:38.639
healthcare or FinTech or what have you.

00:21:38.639 --> 00:21:42.261
I'm curious, obviously you had
some great conversations and

00:21:42.261 --> 00:21:45.403
some great points of contact,
but what was the best and

00:21:45.403 --> 00:21:48.426
maybe worst advice that
you got around regulation.

00:21:48.426 --> 00:21:50.430
&gt;&gt; Was, interesting the night before.

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:55.052
Like the night before I got my FDA
warning letter I was actually at

00:21:55.052 --> 00:21:59.599
a healthcare political dinner,
I wouldn't say which group.

00:21:59.599 --> 00:22:03.507
But it was like a group and they were
like, Anne you're getting so stale,

00:22:03.507 --> 00:22:05.900
you should do something more crazy.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:10.105
And I just want to say that was
a group of healthcare regulators, etc,

00:22:10.105 --> 00:22:13.590
who were like,
you gotta keep pushing boundaries.

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:15.076
And then the next day I
got my warning letter.

00:22:15.076 --> 00:22:19.150
So, clearly that team was
pushing in the wrong direction.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:24.112
I mean, look,
I think the number one thing that,

00:22:24.112 --> 00:22:28.954
the best advice we got when
we had our letter was,

00:22:28.954 --> 00:22:32.750
you're on the right side of history.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:35.914
And so it's just a matter of time.

00:22:35.914 --> 00:22:39.782
So one thing I remind people in the
company is that when we started 23andMe,

00:22:39.782 --> 00:22:41.160
gay marriage was illegal.

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:45.596
So, the world does change.

00:22:45.596 --> 00:22:48.527
So, and it changes because of advocacy,

00:22:48.527 --> 00:22:52.870
because of people rising up and
also because of data.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:56.840
So when people ask,
how did 23andMe convince the FDA?

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:58.224
We convinced them with data.

00:22:58.224 --> 00:23:01.776
We went head to head with their
arguments and we're like,

00:23:01.776 --> 00:23:06.540
we're going to prove out that we
are accurate, that people can get it.

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:11.821
So, I think the most important advice for
people is knowing that whatever

00:23:11.821 --> 00:23:16.860
the laws are now or whatever
the regulations are, things can change.

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:21.625
And society is constantly changing, but
you have to put together the right kind of

00:23:21.625 --> 00:23:26.500
package, and the right data, and
the right support to drive that change.

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:28.760
But it's totally mutable.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:35.223
&gt;&gt; One obvious big societal change in the
past three years has been the pandemic.

00:23:35.223 --> 00:23:39.656
And I'm curious how COVID-19
has informed your strategy for

00:23:39.656 --> 00:23:44.099
how 23andMe can engage in
potentially future health crises.

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:50.556
&gt;&gt; Well, I think what was interesting
during the pandemic is because we have

00:23:50.556 --> 00:23:56.218
millions of people who are consented for
research who are part of it, Very rapidly,

00:23:56.218 --> 00:24:02.186
we were able to put out
a questionnaire to customers and

00:24:02.186 --> 00:24:05.836
collect hundreds of thousands.

00:24:05.836 --> 00:24:09.821
And now I think we have like 1.5 million
people who've taken our COVID survey.

00:24:09.821 --> 00:24:14.366
So very quickly we were able to actually
do research on why are some people

00:24:14.366 --> 00:24:17.881
getting so sick,
why are some people not getting sick?

00:24:17.881 --> 00:24:21.447
Even now I'm kind of fascinated,
there must be genetics,

00:24:21.447 --> 00:24:24.231
some people must be genetically resistant.

00:24:24.231 --> 00:24:26.309
And you see that with things like HIV.

00:24:26.309 --> 00:24:30.821
HIV there's actually a mutation where
people are resistant to getting it.

00:24:30.821 --> 00:24:35.562
So we have the ability to very
quickly survey millions of people and

00:24:35.562 --> 00:24:37.690
get information about them.

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:42.225
So in this DNA, it's like it's
kind of remarkable that there's no

00:24:42.225 --> 00:24:45.821
nationwide surveillance system for
public health.

00:24:45.821 --> 00:24:50.003
And we have that ability
now to really do that.

00:24:50.003 --> 00:24:55.604
&gt;&gt; Another aspect of COVID that changed
how leaders interact was I heard a lot

00:24:55.604 --> 00:25:01.311
of CEOs having text chats or email
exchanges with other business leaders.

00:25:01.311 --> 00:25:05.690
And I'm curious how you potentially
leaned on other business leaders and

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:09.020
what kind of maybe business
community that you formed?

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:10.571
&gt;&gt; I mean, in some ways,

00:25:10.571 --> 00:25:16.222
it's been kind of nice like I never had
really the CEO business community before.

00:25:16.222 --> 00:25:20.002
But I feel like there's now
where I do see more CEOs and

00:25:20.002 --> 00:25:25.143
we all have kind of that same pain
point of like, it's really hard.

00:25:25.143 --> 00:25:31.027
And specifically right now it's really
hard, it's a really tight job market.

00:25:31.027 --> 00:25:33.101
So people all want a lot more money.

00:25:33.101 --> 00:25:38.111
And they return to office is
super complicated, is it hybrid?

00:25:38.111 --> 00:25:39.771
Is it in person?

00:25:39.771 --> 00:25:42.584
Everyone wants something different and

00:25:42.584 --> 00:25:45.737
then the stock market
has really collapsed.

00:25:45.737 --> 00:25:48.765
And so there's just a tremendous
amount of uncertainty.

00:25:48.765 --> 00:25:54.433
And also how do you develop
culture in a Zoom environment?

00:25:54.433 --> 00:25:59.399
You can't, you all saw what it was like
going to school in a Zoom environment,

00:25:59.399 --> 00:26:00.385
it's not fun.

00:26:00.385 --> 00:26:05.281
And so having your office be a Zoom
environment is also not fun.

00:26:05.281 --> 00:26:10.451
So, I think it's super complicated,
so like right now it is fun.

00:26:10.451 --> 00:26:13.651
It's kind of relieving with
other CEOs to be like,

00:26:13.651 --> 00:26:15.794
none of us really know what to do.

00:26:15.794 --> 00:26:21.718
And so [LAUGH] there's comfort when we
all have that pain of, it's hard and

00:26:21.718 --> 00:26:26.893
all the market dynamic,
everything is about being redefined.

00:26:26.893 --> 00:26:29.531
Work environments are never
going to be the same.

00:26:29.531 --> 00:26:34.007
And so how do you really
have a hybrid environment?

00:26:34.007 --> 00:26:37.693
So that's been the number one thing
I think for me and even at different

00:26:37.693 --> 00:26:42.025
policies, talking to companies about,
what are you doing for return to office?

00:26:42.025 --> 00:26:44.218
Are people coming in?

00:26:44.218 --> 00:26:46.099
How do you manage vaccinations?

00:26:46.099 --> 00:26:51.113
Everything has been sort of
non stop of helping manage.

00:26:51.113 --> 00:26:52.775
And then also we've had all
kinds of social unrest.

00:26:52.775 --> 00:26:57.619
So, also then how do you
manage George Floyd?

00:26:57.619 --> 00:27:02.419
And now you have Roe versus Wade,
all of political issues are also

00:27:02.419 --> 00:27:06.531
now really rising up to
really occupy people's minds.

00:27:06.531 --> 00:27:09.860
So how do you actually
also have a company and

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:13.462
in the background have
such political issues?

00:27:13.462 --> 00:27:17.696
So I do think that as the world's
gotten more complicated,

00:27:17.696 --> 00:27:21.688
there's been more bonding over,
it's complicated.

00:27:21.688 --> 00:27:26.247
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so much of what we learn at
the GSB is around collaboration and

00:27:26.247 --> 00:27:29.129
doing projects together and everything.

00:27:29.129 --> 00:27:33.945
So I think it's really nice to hear
that that continues even when you're at

00:27:33.945 --> 00:27:34.564
the top.

00:27:34.564 --> 00:27:41.317
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean I think it's
important to always reach out.

00:27:41.317 --> 00:27:45.141
And I have to say LinkedIn is
amazing in this capacity, it's so

00:27:45.141 --> 00:27:47.174
easy to connect with people now.

00:27:47.174 --> 00:27:49.281
And it's so
lovely to go across industries.

00:27:49.281 --> 00:27:52.677
So, I do think that people
are particularly interested.

00:27:52.677 --> 00:27:54.628
It's so dynamic right now.

00:27:54.628 --> 00:27:57.571
People are particularly interested
in hearing different viewpoints.

00:27:57.571 --> 00:28:01.871
So I do think it's like
a particularly receptive time.

00:28:01.871 --> 00:28:04.225
&gt;&gt; You mentioned company
culture briefly and

00:28:04.225 --> 00:28:08.549
every single guest that we've had on
stage has talked about company culture.

00:28:08.549 --> 00:28:10.184
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; It's obviously very important.

00:28:10.184 --> 00:28:14.691
And how were you intentional about
setting the culture at 23, I mean?

00:28:14.691 --> 00:28:19.622
&gt;&gt; Well, I had coming from Wall Street,
and again I lived in Wall Street during

00:28:19.622 --> 00:28:24.168
like kind of the craziest era,
99, 2000, most of you probably

00:28:24.168 --> 00:28:28.408
don't remember this but it was really
&gt;&gt; Wolf of Wall Street.

00:28:28.408 --> 00:28:30.959
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for sure [LAUGH] I mean,

00:28:30.959 --> 00:28:36.785
there's experiences when I see that or
even when I watch billions.

00:28:36.785 --> 00:28:39.603
I was like subparts of billions
are based on real experiences.

00:28:39.603 --> 00:28:43.283
There was one episode I was watching I
was like, I remember when that happened.

00:28:43.283 --> 00:28:47.752
And they got down to
the specifics of specific trades,

00:28:47.752 --> 00:28:52.623
like trades and information,
it was a totally crazy time.

00:28:52.623 --> 00:28:58.779
So in some ways, having that experience, I
learned a lot of things of what not to do.

00:28:58.779 --> 00:29:03.290
And I have one boss who is in jail.

00:29:03.290 --> 00:29:04.921
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]

00:29:04.921 --> 00:29:05.453
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:29:05.453 --> 00:29:07.930
&gt;&gt; And I always say it was like, well,

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:13.143
there's no better way to experience
what not to do, then, leaving that.

00:29:13.143 --> 00:29:18.401
And I had another boss who would get so
angry and would slam phones into the wall.

00:29:18.401 --> 00:29:23.493
And so
it was also an interesting time watching

00:29:23.493 --> 00:29:28.201
Google start and Google being really fun.

00:29:28.201 --> 00:29:30.491
It was super supportive,
it was really quirky.

00:29:30.491 --> 00:29:33.781
They hired my sister
when she was pregnant.

00:29:33.781 --> 00:29:37.494
People wore whatever they wanted.

00:29:37.494 --> 00:29:39.051
They were always rollerblading.

00:29:39.051 --> 00:29:42.881
Sergey and Larry were always
rollerblading into meetings.

00:29:42.881 --> 00:29:45.943
So it kind of opened up the door,
I do credit them.

00:29:45.943 --> 00:29:49.726
They opened up the door for
having different types of cultures.

00:29:49.726 --> 00:29:53.593
And I've never been
a person who likes veneers,

00:29:53.593 --> 00:29:57.941
I have this issue,
I really hate things that are fake.

00:29:57.941 --> 00:30:03.940
And I don't like commercials for
that reason and I don't like a plastic

00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:10.241
office place where everyone's dressed
up nicely, I feel it's a veneer.

00:30:10.241 --> 00:30:13.721
So having a culture that was
authentic was really important to me.

00:30:13.721 --> 00:30:19.196
And having a culture where people felt
really comfortable and you had diversity.

00:30:19.196 --> 00:30:22.025
We're a genetics company,
we support diversity.

00:30:22.025 --> 00:30:26.042
The world is amazing because of diversity,
so we want to support diversity.

00:30:26.042 --> 00:30:30.802
And I realized this with myself is,
you hire extraordinary people but

00:30:30.802 --> 00:30:34.691
extraordinary people are not
extraordinary every day.

00:30:34.691 --> 00:30:38.425
And so we're a team and so for
that reason, if you get sick,

00:30:38.425 --> 00:30:40.161
someone can cover for you.

00:30:40.161 --> 00:30:44.925
Or if you're just having a bad month,
it doesn't sink everything and

00:30:44.925 --> 00:30:48.254
people don't get mad at you,
we are a true team.

00:30:48.254 --> 00:30:53.083
And so I've always wanted to have a
culture where we have extraordinary people

00:30:53.083 --> 00:30:54.923
but it's truly supportive.

00:30:54.923 --> 00:30:59.653
And we have this diversity and
kind of an academic environment you're

00:30:59.653 --> 00:31:03.754
always learning,
you have this humility of I know nothing.

00:31:03.754 --> 00:31:09.560
I still do say it feel like I know
nothing, I'm always just trying to learn.

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:13.594
And I look at my scientists and others,

00:31:13.594 --> 00:31:17.391
that humility is just so important.

00:31:17.391 --> 00:31:22.261
So, when I think about culture,
it was really about authenticity.

00:31:22.261 --> 00:31:25.777
And like truly supportive and
supportive, really,

00:31:25.777 --> 00:31:30.070
if I want to hire people and
have them with me for 10 or 15 years,

00:31:30.070 --> 00:31:33.525
you have to be flexible with
like work life balance.

00:31:33.525 --> 00:31:36.077
You have to be flexible as
people have other issues.

00:31:36.077 --> 00:31:41.170
So like a real balanced environment and
one that hire

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:46.974
humble people who are super
eager to learn from each other.

00:31:46.974 --> 00:31:51.174
And for that reason, I have really great
marketing people who know nothing about

00:31:51.174 --> 00:31:56.636
therapeutics, and therapeutics people who
know nothing about CAC to LTV like that,

00:31:56.636 --> 00:32:02.350
it's so interesting to learn and so
I always tell people like join 23andMe.

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:05.835
And you should take advantage of
the fact that you're constantly learning

00:32:05.835 --> 00:32:07.724
really interesting different things.

00:32:07.724 --> 00:32:11.950
So culture for
me I think has to be really intentional.

00:32:11.950 --> 00:32:14.055
And I think it's really important for

00:32:14.055 --> 00:32:18.265
me to have a positive culture where
it's supportive, and I'm really proud

00:32:18.265 --> 00:32:23.470
of like the number of people who have left
23andMe and gone off to start companies.

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:27.803
And especially a huge number of
women who've done it and they,

00:32:27.803 --> 00:32:32.850
again also are really focused
on creating a positive culture.

00:32:32.850 --> 00:32:34.300
So it spreads.

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:36.940
&gt;&gt; Is there maybe
a little specific norm or

00:32:36.940 --> 00:32:42.610
practice that you think represents
the culture well, something you guys do?

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:44.480
&gt;&gt; We're very focused on exercise.

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:48.100
So I never dress like this.

00:32:48.100 --> 00:32:51.850
It was like a big debate for
me, do I wear my shorts?

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:55.580
So I wear shorts, T shirts and
my running shoes every day.

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:59.280
And I totally terrorize
people who take the elevator.

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:02.670
And I [LAUGH] wanted to put up the signs.

00:33:02.670 --> 00:33:06.792
There was a study that came out about hey,
you're sitting in the elevator but

00:33:06.792 --> 00:33:11.260
if you had just walked up those stairs it
would have added 30 seconds to your life.

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:17.160
So, I just think for me exercise and
health for me, it's walk the walk.

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:21.636
We have good food, it's healthy,
we don't have soda,

00:33:21.636 --> 00:33:25.540
we encourage,
we have lots of fitness classes.

00:33:25.540 --> 00:33:30.592
I have a huge number of people, we have a
very intense workout culture, people love.

00:33:30.592 --> 00:33:34.900
So we have intro to working out and
then more of the advanced.

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:37.480
We have lots of yoga.

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:39.920
I do lots of yoga meetings.

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:44.436
We'll put the notes on the floor and
then you'd downward dog and

00:33:44.436 --> 00:33:46.225
you'd read your notes.

00:33:46.225 --> 00:33:49.753
[LAUGH] Hold on one hand, you're in plank.

00:33:49.753 --> 00:33:50.800
&gt;&gt; I mean, I think you can-
&gt;&gt; Like balancing.

00:33:50.800 --> 00:33:54.753
Yeah, you can be super creative with that.

00:33:54.753 --> 00:33:57.389
I think a hallmark of our
culture is the authenticity.

00:33:57.389 --> 00:34:00.373
You can be you and you don't have to,

00:34:00.373 --> 00:34:04.425
there's no veneer that
you're trying to put on.

00:34:04.425 --> 00:34:07.946
And the best thing about being authentic
too is you make mistakes all the time.

00:34:07.946 --> 00:34:12.196
And then people frankly, I think,
trust you more when you make mistakes.

00:34:12.196 --> 00:34:13.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:34:13.900 --> 00:34:17.650
Well, so many of us here are in
healthcare, but many of us are not.

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:20.178
So I'm curious, from your perspective,

00:34:20.178 --> 00:34:24.520
what is a trend in healthcare today
that we should all be aware of?

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:29.330
&gt;&gt; Well, I think the trend that's hard
to ignore is the rise of virtual.

00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:39.070
So when I first went to Health 2.0
It was a scary set of companies.

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:43.440
I mean this is probably 12, 13 years ago.

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:46.760
It just was not starting yet.

00:34:46.760 --> 00:34:49.330
And it was just not starting yet.

00:34:49.330 --> 00:34:55.113
So now, and even pre-pandemic, we used
to meet with all kinds of companies that

00:34:55.113 --> 00:35:00.770
were virtual pharmacy or virtual
telemedicine and it was such a niche.

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:04.362
And 23andMe had talked about,
should we get involved in these areas?

00:35:04.362 --> 00:35:10.744
And I was like, [SOUND] the last thing we
need to do is pioneer another whole path.

00:35:10.744 --> 00:35:14.216
But the reality is pandemic
made virtual a requirement.

00:35:14.216 --> 00:35:20.590
It's overnight,
you had to start using virtual care.

00:35:20.590 --> 00:35:23.050
And pharmacies and laws change.

00:35:23.050 --> 00:35:25.544
So, I mean,
there was an incredible opportunity,

00:35:25.544 --> 00:35:29.910
this is an example where regulation really
changed and it opened up a huge sector.

00:35:29.910 --> 00:35:32.280
So virtual is really interesting.

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:37.686
The thing I'd point on is almost
all of these virtual companies

00:35:37.686 --> 00:35:42.182
are focused on employer and
health plan based care.

00:35:42.182 --> 00:35:43.794
And that to me always feels odd.

00:35:43.794 --> 00:35:48.903
If you go and you join Amazon for
two years and you're prediabetic and

00:35:48.903 --> 00:35:54.648
you have a program, why do you lose access
to that program if you change jobs?

00:35:54.648 --> 00:35:58.110
Why your health care is with your
employer really doesn't make sense to me.

00:35:58.110 --> 00:36:02.560
So part of what we've always tried to
do is create a world that's outside,

00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:05.680
that you control that's
outside of the system.

00:36:05.680 --> 00:36:08.864
And I would say then other
trend is that more and

00:36:08.864 --> 00:36:12.386
more is truly going towards
your own empowerment.

00:36:12.386 --> 00:36:17.192
There's an act called 21st Century Cures
that empowers you to be able

00:36:17.192 --> 00:36:22.040
to get access to all of your health
records and move them around.

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:25.406
And they're not making it fully easy yet,
but it's coming,

00:36:25.406 --> 00:36:28.480
where you should be able
to own all of your records.

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:31.660
But there's also now price transparency.

00:36:31.660 --> 00:36:36.426
There's really interesting companies now
surfacing that you can actually know if

00:36:36.426 --> 00:36:39.960
you need to go and get a mammogram,
what does it cost?

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:44.356
So in the past, I would call around and
try to get direct to consumer pricing for

00:36:44.356 --> 00:36:46.720
something and it was impossible.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:51.160
And now, it's actually these laws
are in place, to actually force it.

00:36:51.160 --> 00:36:53.750
So I'm a huge proponent.

00:36:53.750 --> 00:36:57.710
I think there's really exciting
direct to consumer worlds.

00:36:57.710 --> 00:37:02.983
And most companies are still focused on
employer or the Medicare populations,

00:37:02.983 --> 00:37:07.715
but I would say that there's a huge
opportunity on direct to consumer.

00:37:07.715 --> 00:37:12.214
And it's just harder because I
think there is a real shift that

00:37:12.214 --> 00:37:14.559
people have to get educated on.

00:37:14.559 --> 00:37:17.946
And I think one thing again,
just to emphasize on healthcare and again,

00:37:17.946 --> 00:37:20.240
privacy is top of mind for lots of people.

00:37:20.240 --> 00:37:24.303
People don't realize that if your
insurance company pays for you to

00:37:24.303 --> 00:37:30.100
do a procedure or something, they then
know that and they own that information.

00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:32.509
So if you don't want your physician or

00:37:32.509 --> 00:37:37.430
your insurance company to know something,
then you should self pay.

00:37:37.430 --> 00:37:42.790
And so part of again like 23andMe I always
say is proudly not HIPAA compliant.

00:37:42.790 --> 00:37:46.820
Because it actually forces us to get your
bill consent, versus like HIPAA now,

00:37:46.820 --> 00:37:49.889
frankly, even I'm always
criticizing Stanford for this,

00:37:49.889 --> 00:37:52.672
you don't have a way of opting
out of their HIPAA form.

00:37:52.672 --> 00:37:56.137
So that means they can take,
I just went to a doctor the other day,

00:37:56.137 --> 00:37:58.279
and they mandate taking your picture and

00:37:58.279 --> 00:38:02.195
you have to sign a consent form that
they can do anything they want with it.

00:38:02.195 --> 00:38:04.638
Really?
Can you imagine that if Facebook's like,

00:38:04.638 --> 00:38:08.388
I'm going to take your picture, and then
I have the ability to do anything I want

00:38:08.388 --> 00:38:10.370
with it and you have to consent for that?

00:38:10.370 --> 00:38:11.880
That's outrage.

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:15.472
So I do think people,
privacy, and healthcare,

00:38:15.472 --> 00:38:18.670
people don't fully understand it.

00:38:18.670 --> 00:38:21.993
And you have GDPR and the California
privacy act that's happened for

00:38:21.993 --> 00:38:23.490
the tech companies.

00:38:23.490 --> 00:38:26.599
But I think there's going to be
a change at some point in healthcare,

00:38:26.599 --> 00:38:28.495
people just haven't woken up to it yet.

00:38:28.495 --> 00:38:31.537
But there's like a really
terrifying world of how everything,

00:38:31.537 --> 00:38:34.930
all your health information is shared
that you just don't know about.

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:38.301
&gt;&gt; That's very helpful advice.

00:38:38.301 --> 00:38:42.288
And speaking of advice,
[COUGH] half of us in this auditorium

00:38:42.288 --> 00:38:46.450
are actually graduating in three weeks,
including myself.

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:47.200
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:52.963
&gt;&gt; And many of us have been considering or
are going to be pivoting their careers.

00:38:52.963 --> 00:38:58.030
You've pivoted a few times and I'm curious
what lessons you have to share with us.

00:38:58.030 --> 00:39:00.710
&gt;&gt; I think pivoting is
the best thing ever.

00:39:00.710 --> 00:39:07.360
I think it's so
interesting to learn different areas.

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:11.397
I don't know if you knew Larry Lessig,
who's used to be at Stanford who ran like

00:39:11.397 --> 00:39:15.013
Internet law and he was just one day,
I want to learn about corruption and

00:39:15.013 --> 00:39:19.075
he moved to Harvard and he's like, I'm
going to become an expert in corruption.

00:39:19.075 --> 00:39:24.460
It's so great, to do the same thing
all the time, it's just boring.

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:29.206
So to do totally different things,
the world is so interesting, you should

00:39:29.206 --> 00:39:34.640
absolutely keep pushing yourself to just
keep pivot and do interesting things.

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:37.644
I always encourage, I mean,
I try to keep most people at 23andMe, but

00:39:37.644 --> 00:39:41.093
when someone's actually been there for a
while and they want to try something else,

00:39:41.093 --> 00:39:43.660
and be like, yeah,
you should quit and do something new.

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:48.483
The world is so interesting,
never shortchange yourself on going and

00:39:48.483 --> 00:39:49.390
exploring.

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:54.298
You guys are all super, I mean, it's an
amazing talent pool, everyone can go and

00:39:54.298 --> 00:39:56.220
get interesting job.

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:57.795
You owe it to yourself to go and

00:39:57.795 --> 00:40:01.560
have that exploration of totally
different types of careers.

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:04.412
And frankly,
I feel grateful to my parents who,

00:40:04.412 --> 00:40:09.050
when we graduated college, there was
no push to go and get a single job.

00:40:09.050 --> 00:40:15.560
So I worked as a nanny, I temped, I worked
in all kinds of different random areas.

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:19.768
And all of those experiences were super
helpful of knowing what you really want.

00:40:19.768 --> 00:40:23.324
And even obviously, Wall Street
was just a short term for me, and

00:40:23.324 --> 00:40:25.596
I think I'll be at 23andMe for a while.

00:40:25.596 --> 00:40:29.281
But I have this luxury of, I'm always
doing different things with 23andMe,

00:40:29.281 --> 00:40:31.280
I'm now in drug discovery more.

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:36.126
And then personally I do all these real
estate projects at home in Los Altos,

00:40:36.126 --> 00:40:38.030
that's super interesting.

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:42.751
So I totally encourage, keep pivoting,
and every experience adds up.

00:40:42.751 --> 00:40:48.108
And you never know when an experience
is going to be valuable,

00:40:48.108 --> 00:40:51.825
and if it's not interesting, then quit.

00:40:51.825 --> 00:40:53.842
&gt;&gt; Great, well that was definitely
an answer I needed to hear.

00:40:53.842 --> 00:40:54.739
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:40:54.739 --> 00:40:56.301
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So thank you.

00:40:56.301 --> 00:40:57.199
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Definitely.

00:40:57.199 --> 00:40:58.903
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well, thank you Ann,

00:40:58.903 --> 00:41:03.865
I deeply admire the conviction that
you've had throughout your entire career.

00:41:03.865 --> 00:41:07.290
And I know all of us here
are excited to see how you and

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:10.413
23andMe will continue to change the world.

00:41:10.413 --> 00:41:13.951
And I have more questions, but I know
we have some questions in the audience.

00:41:13.951 --> 00:41:15.362
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; I'd like to turn it over to them.

00:41:15.362 --> 00:41:16.154
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:41:21.441 --> 00:41:26.050
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Neil Rands, I'm a
medical student and business student here.

00:41:26.050 --> 00:41:31.757
And I'm curious how you think about
balancing data privacy, patient

00:41:31.757 --> 00:41:38.249
privacy with sharing information in
a way that advances scientific progress?

00:41:39.830 --> 00:41:44.653
&gt;&gt; I love the privacy questions,
because again,

00:41:44.653 --> 00:41:48.992
I think that what I found
is that most people

00:41:48.992 --> 00:41:53.880
actually want to participate in research.

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:56.905
So when we started the company,
it was sort of hypothesized,

00:41:56.905 --> 00:41:59.710
people don't actually want to
participate in research.

00:41:59.710 --> 00:42:02.012
It was almost this mindset
of a clinical trial.

00:42:02.012 --> 00:42:03.960
Get them to come in and
consent for everything,

00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:07.170
because you never know if you'll be
able to really get anything else.

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:11.041
And I looked at things like
Susan G Komen and Livestrong and

00:42:11.041 --> 00:42:13.254
all this enthusiasm to do walks.

00:42:13.254 --> 00:42:16.092
People really actually
want to make a difference and

00:42:16.092 --> 00:42:18.660
even bone marrow drives and such.

00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:23.048
And I go again to some of these
human subjects consent forms,

00:42:23.048 --> 00:42:27.609
where they don't learn information
back to individuals, and

00:42:27.609 --> 00:42:30.379
frankly I find them super insulting.

00:42:30.379 --> 00:42:37.260
It's the paternalistic side, you think I'm
not capable of getting this information?

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:43.310
And just to level set with people, there's
a JAMA study from I think 1960 where they

00:42:43.310 --> 00:42:48.809
asked physicians whether or not they would
tell a patient that they had cancer.

00:42:48.809 --> 00:42:53.104
And 90% of physicians said they
would not tell their patient.

00:42:53.104 --> 00:42:57.078
So the world has really evolved and
it's come a long ways and

00:42:57.078 --> 00:43:00.746
I think it's kudos to
the medical world for evolving.

00:43:00.746 --> 00:43:05.657
But I think that we can absolutely
do more with looking at individuals

00:43:05.657 --> 00:43:10.314
as a responsible individual who
can handle their information and

00:43:10.314 --> 00:43:12.730
more as a partnership.

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:18.133
And so right now, a lot of healthcare
providers and insurance companies and

00:43:18.133 --> 00:43:24.230
Epic, they all use HIPAA as a way to not
let a consumer share their information.

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:29.374
There's always these concerns, a consumer
might get their medical records and

00:43:29.374 --> 00:43:33.947
then accidentally share it in the wrong
way, do the wrong things with it, or

00:43:33.947 --> 00:43:36.536
it might be too much information for them.

00:43:36.536 --> 00:43:39.438
There's kind of a very
paternalistic take on it.

00:43:39.438 --> 00:43:44.166
And I've just always felt you should
be entitled to your information.

00:43:44.166 --> 00:43:48.962
And then put it on the individual,
if you want to share it in your ways,

00:43:48.962 --> 00:43:51.230
like allow you to share it.

00:43:51.230 --> 00:43:53.271
So to answer your question specifically,

00:43:53.271 --> 00:43:56.965
I think that there should be transparency
and I think there should be choice.

00:43:56.965 --> 00:44:01.288
That's the hallmark of everything 23andMe
does, is I'm fiercely independent and

00:44:01.288 --> 00:44:03.910
I never want someone
telling me what to do.

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:06.230
And the last thing I want someone tell,

00:44:06.230 --> 00:44:09.761
my health care provider should
not be telling me what to do.

00:44:09.761 --> 00:44:13.056
Give me the information and
I will make my own decisions.

00:44:13.056 --> 00:44:16.023
And 80% of our customers
opt in to research.

00:44:16.023 --> 00:44:18.624
It's not even they have
to click out of it,

00:44:18.624 --> 00:44:21.170
they're electing to opt in to research.

00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:22.674
So I would argue that,

00:44:22.674 --> 00:44:27.120
even more people would opt in to
research if it was given a choice.

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.756
And that was kind of my
point with Stanford,

00:44:29.756 --> 00:44:33.796
why aren't I giving a choice of
actually opting in or opting out?

00:44:33.796 --> 00:44:35.790
Why do I have to be part of it?

00:44:35.790 --> 00:44:37.777
And so I do think that choice and

00:44:37.777 --> 00:44:41.170
transparency is actually
how you build trust.

00:44:41.170 --> 00:44:44.289
And I think a lot of consumers
actually really want to be part of it.

00:44:44.289 --> 00:44:46.419
But also one more thing
I would put on you is,

00:44:46.419 --> 00:44:48.850
people should have the right
to know the results.

00:44:49.920 --> 00:44:54.110
So if you participated in the study,
tell people what came out of that study.

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:58.716
Most people participate in a clinical
trial and they have no idea what happened,

00:44:58.716 --> 00:44:59.876
isn't that crazy?

00:44:59.876 --> 00:45:03.880
Again, I find it totally insulting,
don't treat me like a human subject,

00:45:03.880 --> 00:45:05.205
treat me like a partner.

00:45:14.914 --> 00:45:20.800
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Harshit and I'm from India,
I'm studying, business here.

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:24.918
I want to understand how you have used, or

00:45:24.918 --> 00:45:31.400
maybe how you have insights from
genetics on racial inequality?

00:45:31.400 --> 00:45:33.992
&gt;&gt; I mean, looking at diversity,
I mean, so

00:45:33.992 --> 00:45:37.880
it's a question there's lots
of complexities your question.

00:45:37.880 --> 00:45:42.629
I would say a couple of things one,
when we started 23andMe,

00:45:42.629 --> 00:45:47.660
we never expected that we're going to
be in the dialogue about race.

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:50.479
We got into it for research and
all these things and

00:45:50.479 --> 00:45:53.110
suddenly we're about redefining race.

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:57.855
We absolutely can see, we had one
really interesting publication where we

00:45:57.855 --> 00:46:01.104
asked people the definition
of African American.

00:46:01.104 --> 00:46:05.942
And you can see how people in the South
might have 10% African DNA and

00:46:05.942 --> 00:46:08.902
they do not identify as African American.

00:46:08.902 --> 00:46:13.391
And you have people in Seattle who
have zero percentage African DNA and

00:46:13.391 --> 00:46:16.170
they identify as African American.

00:46:16.170 --> 00:46:19.910
So even defining what you are and
your environment where you are,

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:21.850
it's super complicated.

00:46:21.850 --> 00:46:26.385
So there's absolutely aspects
that I think 23andMe can

00:46:26.385 --> 00:46:31.105
have an impact on by helping
understand genetic ancestry and

00:46:31.105 --> 00:46:36.890
likelihood to respond to medication or
likelihood to have an issue.

00:46:36.890 --> 00:46:40.763
So for instance, there's someone
named Carlos Bustamante at UCSF, and

00:46:40.763 --> 00:46:44.270
he does phenomenal research in
the Latino population and asthma.

00:46:44.270 --> 00:46:48.510
And he has seen there's certain
medications that people should never take

00:46:48.510 --> 00:46:51.750
in the Latino population,
but why are they prescribed?

00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:55.490
And you have another
example where in Hawaii,

00:46:55.490 --> 00:46:58.530
the state of Hawaii does not
allow the sale of Plavix.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:03.907
Plavix, again, is a well known as
an anti-clotting drug, and it's because so

00:47:03.907 --> 00:47:07.350
many people in the state don't
respond to the medication.

00:47:07.350 --> 00:47:11.908
So I think one way, again, it's racial and
health inequity is a huge issue.

00:47:11.908 --> 00:47:16.330
But I think one thing that you can
actually start to do is really understand

00:47:16.330 --> 00:47:21.110
not racial profiling people, but really
understand your genetic ancestry and

00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:23.680
what the mutations are that you have.

00:47:23.680 --> 00:47:27.352
And then whether or not you have
a predisposition for a condition and

00:47:27.352 --> 00:47:29.615
should you be managed in a different way?

00:47:29.615 --> 00:47:32.915
Or whether or not you have
a predisposition to not respond or

00:47:32.915 --> 00:47:34.899
likely to respond to a medication.

00:47:34.899 --> 00:47:38.913
So I do think there's a ton of
opportunity to have improvement.

00:47:38.913 --> 00:47:41.446
And again, racial profiling's only,

00:47:41.446 --> 00:47:45.328
it's the last area where it's
acceptable is in healthcare.

00:47:45.328 --> 00:47:51.203
It's the first line of every medical
record, 45 year old white male comes in.

00:47:51.203 --> 00:47:54.143
It's not something I think that we
actually really have the opportunity

00:47:54.143 --> 00:47:54.730
of changing.

00:48:03.123 --> 00:48:04.081
Hi, how's it going?

00:48:04.081 --> 00:48:05.680
My name is Jammas.

00:48:05.680 --> 00:48:08.151
Like Neil, I'm also a MD MBA student.

00:48:08.151 --> 00:48:10.820
&gt;&gt; I love it.
&gt;&gt; And I worked in a Huntington's disease

00:48:10.820 --> 00:48:13.527
clinic for a year before medical school.

00:48:13.527 --> 00:48:16.973
And a lot of times the genetic results
that they would get was some of the most

00:48:16.973 --> 00:48:19.775
devastating news they could
ever hear in their entire life.

00:48:19.775 --> 00:48:24.465
And how would you respond to people
who believe 23andMe should have a more

00:48:24.465 --> 00:48:29.382
thorough informed consent process
before releasing information like BRCA1

00:48:29.382 --> 00:48:31.182
mutations, for instance?

00:48:31.182 --> 00:48:36.094
And then do you foresee a future
where people might push to have

00:48:36.094 --> 00:48:40.535
patients act on some of
the information they receive and

00:48:40.535 --> 00:48:46.222
how are you preparing for that,
once those treatments are available?

00:48:46.222 --> 00:48:49.670
&gt;&gt; So again it goes to some of
the paternalistic questions.

00:48:49.670 --> 00:48:58.346
I think that you can absolutely have
a great informed consent online.

00:48:58.346 --> 00:49:01.412
And I think that's the kind of
thing that's like,

00:49:01.412 --> 00:49:04.187
it should not be a debate,
it's about data.

00:49:04.187 --> 00:49:08.244
Generate data where you ask people to fill
up the online consent and then you ask

00:49:08.244 --> 00:49:12.382
them to go through counseling, and
then you have them answer those questions.

00:49:12.382 --> 00:49:13.970
Can you understand it?

00:49:13.970 --> 00:49:15.668
So we've done a lot of work about,

00:49:15.668 --> 00:49:18.140
are people understanding
what they are getting?

00:49:19.710 --> 00:49:22.933
So I would argue that having
an online consent and

00:49:22.933 --> 00:49:25.693
making sure is potentially even better.

00:49:25.693 --> 00:49:30.104
So for instance, 23andMe,
you go through a number of consents.

00:49:30.104 --> 00:49:34.920
You can't sign up accidentally,
it's a hard process.

00:49:34.920 --> 00:49:37.389
Reports like the Alzheimer's BRCA for

00:49:37.389 --> 00:49:41.068
breast cancer are behind
an additional layer of consent.

00:49:41.068 --> 00:49:42.765
So you have to click through and

00:49:42.765 --> 00:49:47.040
there's a video that talks
about what potentially happens.

00:49:47.040 --> 00:49:51.149
There's all kinds of resources about, hey,
here's the information you might learn,

00:49:51.149 --> 00:49:52.262
this is what to expect.

00:49:52.262 --> 00:49:55.079
And we see people pause on health.

00:49:55.079 --> 00:49:57.185
And not everyone wants to know health.

00:49:57.185 --> 00:50:02.073
And to your point on Huntington's,
the information is real.

00:50:02.073 --> 00:50:04.336
It's meaningful for people.

00:50:04.336 --> 00:50:08.597
But Huntington's is a slightly
different story because you

00:50:08.597 --> 00:50:10.692
can't do anything about it.

00:50:10.692 --> 00:50:14.317
Verses in BRCA, at a certain age, you can.

00:50:14.317 --> 00:50:17.493
You can find out that
you are high risk and

00:50:17.493 --> 00:50:21.430
get a prophylactic mastectomy and
ovariectomy.

00:50:22.830 --> 00:50:26.920
And we've seen people, we have thousands
of customers who have now gotten this.

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:31.431
And we can see that 20 to 30% of
our customers never would have

00:50:31.431 --> 00:50:35.350
qualified under existing
insurance guidelines.

00:50:35.350 --> 00:50:40.225
So I would argue that healthcare
has huge limitations because

00:50:40.225 --> 00:50:42.950
we have not enabled broad access.

00:50:42.950 --> 00:50:49.783
And so saying that you can't get this
information until you have a one-to-one,

00:50:49.783 --> 00:50:55.440
in-person kind of interaction,
only puts up barriers to access.

00:50:55.440 --> 00:50:57.826
For me also, the fundamental premise and
actually learn this,

00:50:57.826 --> 00:51:00.100
I would spend a lot of
time investing in India.

00:51:00.100 --> 00:51:04.727
Healthcare will never be accessible
to all if it's dependent upon

00:51:04.727 --> 00:51:06.884
a one-to-one interaction.

00:51:06.884 --> 00:51:12.199
That right there should be a good
concept for all of you to run on.

00:51:12.199 --> 00:51:14.653
You can't really improve care for

00:51:14.653 --> 00:51:18.634
people unless we find ways to
automate it more and more.

00:51:18.634 --> 00:51:19.692
And so that has to happen.

00:51:19.692 --> 00:51:23.689
In the first company I ever invested in,
talk about things that were too early,

00:51:23.689 --> 00:51:26.780
it was a pap smear company
called Neuromedical.

00:51:26.780 --> 00:51:29.413
And it was one of the first AI
companies that I had ever seen.

00:51:29.413 --> 00:51:31.079
It was back in 1996.

00:51:31.079 --> 00:51:34.478
And they would take pap smears,
the majority of which are normal, and

00:51:34.478 --> 00:51:37.817
they'd run them through the machine,
and then they would isolate,

00:51:37.817 --> 00:51:40.715
here is the top 20% that we
need a pathologist to look at.

00:51:40.715 --> 00:51:43.660
And the company got taken
down by the pathologist.

00:51:43.660 --> 00:51:46.047
How dare you automate our job?

00:51:46.047 --> 00:51:50.002
But the reality is,
you can't have scalable solutions

00:51:50.002 --> 00:51:54.137
in healthcare if you don't
have automation in some ways.

00:51:54.137 --> 00:51:57.366
I ask my kids sometimes, all of you here,
you probably don't remember.

00:51:57.366 --> 00:52:01.255
When I was little and
I wanted to book a hotel in Paris,

00:52:01.255 --> 00:52:05.846
I had to set my alarm, and wake up and
call, and talk to a human.

00:52:05.846 --> 00:52:07.071
That's how it worked.

00:52:07.071 --> 00:52:08.673
And now we're all totally spoiled.

00:52:08.673 --> 00:52:10.903
You go online, you can just book,
it's all automated.

00:52:10.903 --> 00:52:12.640
We never talk to people.

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:15.935
And so, healthcare has to go more and
more in that direction.

00:52:15.935 --> 00:52:19.873
And then save the really
expensive precious resources for

00:52:19.873 --> 00:52:22.535
people who really, really need care.

00:52:28.774 --> 00:52:31.191
&gt;&gt; We have time for one more.

00:52:31.191 --> 00:52:32.276
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Brianna.

00:52:32.276 --> 00:52:36.090
I'm a geneticist and
actually a law student here.

00:52:36.090 --> 00:52:40.460
And so talking about privacy and consent.

00:52:40.460 --> 00:52:43.995
The thing that's different about genetic
information is, it's not just about you,

00:52:43.995 --> 00:52:45.290
it's about your family.

00:52:45.290 --> 00:52:49.401
So how do you think about your
responsibility for community consent or

00:52:49.401 --> 00:52:51.632
familial consent, and community and

00:52:51.632 --> 00:52:55.020
familial understanding of
the data that you collect?

00:52:55.020 --> 00:52:58.290
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:52:58.290 --> 00:53:00.890
And it's a huge complexity here.

00:53:02.240 --> 00:53:05.917
So we have gone with the hypothesis,
it's your DNA,

00:53:05.917 --> 00:53:12.350
it's your right to learn about yourself,
but there are absolutely consequences.

00:53:12.350 --> 00:53:14.482
And so, for instance,

00:53:14.482 --> 00:53:20.566
your mother might not want to learn
that if she's a carrier for BRCA.

00:53:20.566 --> 00:53:26.175
And you could get tested and
find out that you have two copies.

00:53:26.175 --> 00:53:30.959
And therefore it's 100%
deterministic your mother has it.

00:53:30.959 --> 00:53:33.996
And we've seen that instance.

00:53:33.996 --> 00:53:37.636
And we see this, I would say,
what's interesting, again,

00:53:37.636 --> 00:53:42.188
I love all the MDs in here, and it'd be
super fun, we should chat more later.

00:53:42.188 --> 00:53:46.704
We don't get lots of consternation and
concern about health reports.

00:53:46.704 --> 00:53:50.868
Our customers, we find,
are mostly, they're okay.

00:53:50.868 --> 00:53:56.493
A BRCA report is not fun to get, but
they can handle all this information.

00:53:56.493 --> 00:53:58.682
And we've proven that now in spades.

00:53:58.682 --> 00:54:03.232
The part that no one anticipates is that
their parent is not their parent, or

00:54:03.232 --> 00:54:05.930
their sibling is not their sibling.

00:54:05.930 --> 00:54:10.134
If you have access to
your genetic information,

00:54:10.134 --> 00:54:14.860
you're going to learn things
about your entire family.

00:54:16.030 --> 00:54:19.340
And so I don't think that there's
a realistic world of group consent.

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:24.454
I think the reality is,
you own the right to your own body.

00:54:24.454 --> 00:54:29.692
But we absolutely try to educate people,
that your information has consequences.

00:54:29.692 --> 00:54:34.350
But I would say, no one ever thinks it's

00:54:34.350 --> 00:54:39.424
them that is not related
to a family member.

00:54:39.424 --> 00:54:43.891
And we have an additional consent form,
that is just always a surprise for people.

00:54:43.891 --> 00:54:48.960
But it's a very real and
very tricky aspect of 23andMe is that,

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:53.360
you're not just related
to your immediate family.

00:54:53.360 --> 00:54:56.623
This whole room is genetically connected.

00:54:56.623 --> 00:55:00.599
And so, any number, you would probably
take some percentage of this room and

00:55:00.599 --> 00:55:02.081
kind of connect everybody.

00:55:02.081 --> 00:55:07.268
So I totally real and that's, in some ways
there's the inspiring part for research.

00:55:07.268 --> 00:55:10.333
In that,
we really are all in this together.

00:55:10.333 --> 00:55:14.908
So if you want to solve the mystery of
the human genome, and you want to solve

00:55:14.908 --> 00:55:19.558
the genetics of various disease, and
what does it really mean to be well,

00:55:19.558 --> 00:55:22.799
it is within the power of
all of us coming together.

00:55:22.799 --> 00:55:26.214
But there's absolutely has to be some
of the ethics, and the consent of,

00:55:26.214 --> 00:55:28.210
how are we actually all getting together?

00:55:28.210 --> 00:55:32.724
And that's what I said, in Stanford
there's a lot, again, Hank who is,

00:55:32.724 --> 00:55:34.955
again, engaged with us quite a bit.

00:55:34.955 --> 00:55:37.468
But there's really interesting
questions around this, around ethics.

00:55:37.468 --> 00:55:41.054
And that's why, again,
I'm eternally grateful to Joanna Mountain,

00:55:41.054 --> 00:55:44.889
when she came from Stanford, she was
absolutely a thought leader in ethics.

00:55:44.889 --> 00:55:48.868
And when I always think about
the difference between 23andMe and

00:55:48.868 --> 00:55:53.001
other companies like Facebook,
didn't start with a consent form.

00:55:53.001 --> 00:55:55.034
I know they have a research team and
other things, but

00:55:55.034 --> 00:55:56.578
there's no consent that's part of it.

00:55:56.578 --> 00:56:00.727
23andMe from day one, had consent and
engaged with ethicists and

00:56:00.727 --> 00:56:05.370
really thought through, and
recognize there's consequences.

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:11.363
But in some ways, the guiding principle
for 23andMe is transparency and choice.

00:56:11.363 --> 00:56:15.547
And whether it's transparency in yourself,
it's choice, whether you want to get your

00:56:15.547 --> 00:56:18.840
genetic information, choice if you
want to participate in research,

00:56:18.840 --> 00:56:21.103
choice if you don't want to
do all these things.

00:56:21.103 --> 00:56:23.230
And I think that's one of
the issues I have in healthcare.

00:56:23.230 --> 00:56:29.830
Is most times you're not provided choice
and you're not provided transparency.

00:56:29.830 --> 00:56:33.264
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you, Anne, for
your leadership and your wisdom.

00:56:33.264 --> 00:56:34.071
And thank you all for coming.

00:56:34.071 --> 00:56:35.440
That concludes View From The Top.

00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:39.709
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:56:39.709 --> 00:56:44.907
[MUSIC]

